<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  PRIVACY MANIFEST — the app target's own declaration.

  Apple has required this since May 2024; an upload without one draws ITMS-91053
  ("Missing API declaration"). It covers THIS TARGET'S code only. Third-party SDKs
  ship their own manifests and Xcode aggregates them at build time, so nothing here
  should be describing Firebase or the background-geolocation plugin.

  ADD ONLY WHAT IS TRUE. An over-broad declaration makes the App Store privacy label
  say the app takes more than it does, which is its own kind of dishonesty toward
  members being asked to be located.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>

  <!-- NO TRACKING, and no tracking domains.

       "Tracking" in Apple's sense means linking this app's data to third-party data
       for advertising or measurement, or sharing it with a data broker. B4C does
       neither: location goes to this department's own Supabase instance and nowhere
       else, there is no ad SDK, no analytics SDK, and no attribution network.

       NSPrivacyTrackingDomains MUST stay empty while NSPrivacyTracking is false —
       a non-empty list with tracking false is a contradiction Apple rejects. -->
  <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
  <false/>
  <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
  <array/>

  <!-- REQUIRED-REASON APIs.

       UserDefaults (CA92.1 — "access info from the app itself"), declared
       DEFENSIVELY, and the reasoning is worth stating exactly because the obvious
       justification turned out to be wrong.

       Our own Swift is two files, AppDelegate and SceneDelegate, and neither calls a
       required-reason API. It was first assumed the Capacitor bridge was the reason
       — but Capacitor.framework ships a manifest declaring NO accessed API types at
       all, so that is not it. TSLocationManager declares its own UserDefaults use
       (CA92.1 + 1C8F.1) inside its own framework, which covers the SDK and not us.

       So on the evidence available here, this target may genuinely need no
       accessed-API entry. It is kept because the check Apple runs is static analysis
       of the BUILT BINARY, which cannot be reproduced from source, and the two
       outcomes are not symmetric: an accurate over-declaration of a required-reason
       API costs nothing, while a missing one is the exact cause of ITMS-91053.
       CA92.1 is the correct reason in either case — this app reads and writes only
       its own defaults, never another app's through an app group. -->

  <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
  <array>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
      <array>
        <string>CA92.1</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
  </array>

  <!-- COLLECTED DATA.

       PRECISE LOCATION, and only that. The geofence hands coordinates to
       geofence_arrive/station_check_in, which compare them to the house's pin and
       stamp a boolean.

       WHAT IS ACTUALLY STORED: department_id, member_id, verified, source, kind,
       checked_in_at, station_id. THE COORDINATES ARE NOT PERSISTED — they are used
       to decide `verified` and discarded. That is why there is no coarse-location or
       "other data" entry here, and it is the same promise the location usage strings
       make to the member.

       LINKED TO THE USER: TRUE. The transmission happens in the member's own
       authenticated session and produces a shift row attributed to their member_id.
       Even though the latitude and longitude are never written down, what the app
       learns from them is recorded against a named person, and calling that
       "not linked" would be false. Over-declaring linkage costs a stricter privacy
       label; under-declaring it misrepresents the app to the people it locates.

       USED FOR TRACKING: FALSE — see NSPrivacyTracking above.

       PURPOSE: App Functionality alone. Nothing here feeds analytics, product
       personalisation, or advertising, and no other purpose may be added without a
       change to what the app actually does. -->
  <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
  <array>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePreciseLocation</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
  </array>

</dict>
</plist>
